an input interface adapted to receive a control signal;
an energy conversion mechanism situated within the housing and operably coupled to the input interface, the energy conversion mechanism configured to transform input energy into mechanical work responsive to the control signal; and
an output member mechanically linked to the energy conversion mechanism and projecting from the housing, the output member configured to transmit the mechanical work to an external system.

A catalyst system for a fuel cell electrode, comprising:
a plurality of graphite nanofibers;
a catalytic material chosen from the group consisting of platinum group metals, non-platinum group metals, and combinations thereof, the catalytic material being supported on surfaces of the plurality of graphite nanofibers;
sufficient electrical conductivity for use in a fuel cell electrode; and
wherein the graphite nanofibers impart structural support to the dispersed catalytic material.

A golf ball, comprising:
a central core formed from a first thermoset polymeric material exhibiting a first Shore D hardness in the range of 30 to 50;
an intermediate layer encompassing the central core, said intermediate layer comprising a second polymeric material exhibiting a second Shore D hardness in the range of 55 to 70;
